"""Printed81–83 questions; separately authored progressive learning answers."""
from html import escape
MCQS=[]
def mcq(n,q,opts,ans,why,page=81):MCQS.append(dict(qNo=n,q=q,opts=opts,ans=ans,explanation=why,sourceReferences=[dict(printedPage=page)],solutionOrigin='SpaceBook learning answer'))
mcq('i','The disorder in which bones are porous and thin but bone composition is normal is;',['osteomalacia','osteoporosis','rickets','arthritis'],1,'Osteoporosis involves loss of bone mass and deterioration of its structure, making bones thinner and more fragile.')
mcq('ii',"The organic portion of bone’s matrix is important in providing all but;",['tensile strength','hardness','to resist stretch','flexibility'],1,'Organic collagen contributes flexibility and tensile resistance; mineral salts provide the characteristic hardness.')
mcq('iii','The remodeling of bone is a function of which cells?',['chondrocytes and osteocytes','osteoblasts and osteoclasts','chondroblasts and osteoclasts','osteoblasts and osteocytes'],1,'Osteoclasts resorb old bone and osteoblasts form new bone during remodeling.')
mcq('iv','In skeletal muscle, calcium facilitates contraction by binding to',['tropomyosin','actin.','troponin.','myosin.'],2,'Calcium binds troponin, allowing tropomyosin to shift and expose actin’s myosin-binding sites.')
mcq('v','Which of the following statements concerning the role of Ca²⁺ in the contraction of skeletal muscle is correct?',['The mitochondria act as a store of Ca²⁺ for the contractile process','Ca²⁺ entry across the plasma membrane is important in sustaining the contraction of skeletal muscle','A rise in intracellular Ca²⁺ allows actin to interact with myosin','The tension of a skeletal muscle fibre is partly regulated by G proteins'],2,'Increased calcium in the muscle-fibre cytoplasm permits the actin–myosin interaction through the regulatory proteins.')
mcq('vi','The function of the T tubules in muscle contraction is to',['make and store glycogen','release Ca²⁺ into the cell interior and then pick it up again','make the action potential deep into the muscle cells','to hamper the nerve impulse'],2,'T tubules conduct the action potential into the fibre; the sarcoplasmic reticulum stores, releases and takes up calcium.')
mcq('vii','The sites where the motor nerve impulse is transmitted from the nerve endings to the skeletal muscle cell membranes are the:',['neuromuscular junctions','sarcomeres','myofilaments','Z discs'],0,'A neuromuscular junction is the communication site between a motor neuron and a skeletal muscle fibre.')
mcq('viii','Myoglobin has a special function in muscle tissue.',['it breaks down glycogen','it is a contractile protein','it holds a reserve supply of oxygen in the muscle','none of these'],2,'Myoglobin binds oxygen and provides a reserve in muscle cells.',82)
def solved(n,q,steps,answer):
 html='<div><strong>SpaceBook learning answer</strong><ol>'
 for i,step in enumerate(steps):html+=f'<li data-example-step="{i}" style="'+('display:none;' if i else '')+'">'+escape(step)+'</li>'
 html+='</ol><button type="button" data-revealed="1" onclick="revealMathExampleStep(this)">Next step</button> <button type="button" onclick="resetMathExampleSteps(this)">Start again</button></div><div data-example-answer style="display:none;margin-top:.7rem;"><strong>Answer: </strong>'+escape(answer)+'</div>'
 return dict(qNo=n,q=q,steps=steps,answer=html,plainAnswer=answer,solutionOrigin='SpaceBook learning answer',sourceReferences=[dict(printedPage=82)])
SHORT=[
solved('i','Name the cranial and facial bones.',['Cranial bones: frontal, occipital, sphenoid and ethmoid are unpaired; parietal and temporal are paired. This gives eight cranial bones.','Facial bones: mandible and vomer are unpaired; maxilla, zygomatic, lacrimal, nasal, inferior nasal concha and palatine are paired. This gives fourteen facial bones.'],'Eight cranial bones form the cranium and fourteen facial bones form the face, as listed in Table16.3.'),
solved('ii','What is the function of the intervertebral discs?',['The discs lie between adjacent vertebral bodies.','Their resilient structure absorbs shocks and distributes loads.','They also allow limited movement while helping connect and support the vertebral column.'],'Intervertebral discs cushion the vertebrae, absorb shocks and permit controlled flexibility.'),
solved('iv','Briefly describe the impairment of function seen in cleft palate.',['A cleft palate leaves an opening in the roof of the mouth.','This can make feeding difficult and interfere with normal speech.','Associated middle-ear problems may affect hearing.'],'Cleft palate can impair feeding, speech and hearing; effects vary between individuals.')]
SHORT[-1]['answer']+='<p>Source: <a href="https://www.gosh.nhs.uk/conditions-and-treatments/conditions-we-treat/cleft-lip-and-palate/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Great Ormond Street Hospital — cleft lip and palate</a>.</p>'
LONG=[
solved('i','Describe the structure of bone.',['Bone is living connective tissue containing cells embedded in a hard, mineralized matrix.','Its organic component is chiefly collagen, which contributes flexibility and tensile strength. Inorganic calcium-phosphate-rich mineral gives hardness.','Osteoblasts produce bone matrix; osteocytes are mature bone cells within it; osteoclasts resorb bone during remodeling.','Bone has blood vessels that support its living cells and metabolic exchange.','The chapter gives two approximate organic/inorganic ratios in different paragraphs; use the qualitative distinction between collagen and mineral rather than treating the two printed ratios as identical.'],'Bone consists of living cells in a collagen-and-mineral matrix and is continually maintained and remodeled.'),
solved('ii','Describe major divisions of human axial skeleton',['The skull protects the brain and includes the cranial and facial bones, with associated ear ossicles and hyoid discussed in the chapter.','The vertebral column supports the trunk and protects the spinal cord. Its regions are cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacral and coccygeal.','The thoracic cage consists of ribs and sternum and protects the heart and lungs.','There are twelve rib pairs: seven attach directly to the sternum, three indirectly, and the final two have no anterior attachment.'],'The axial skeleton comprises the skull, vertebral column and thoracic cage.'),
solved('iii','What are types of fractures? Describe the repair process of a simple fracture.',['A simple or closed fracture leaves the skin intact; an open or compound fracture communicates with a skin wound.','Hematoma formation: torn vessels bleed and a clot forms at the break.','Fibrocartilaginous callus formation: new vessels and repair cells enter; a soft callus bridges the broken ends.','Bony callus formation: the soft callus is replaced by a harder bridge of spongy bone.','Remodeling: bone is reorganized in response to mechanical stresses, restoring its structure.'],'The two source fracture types are closed and open; repair proceeds through hematoma, soft callus, bony callus and remodeling.'),
solved('iv','Compare smooth, cardiac, and skeletal muscles.',['Skeletal muscle attaches to the skeleton, has long unbranched striated fibres with many nuclei, and is normally under voluntary control. It moves the body and maintains posture.','Cardiac muscle occurs in the heart, is striated and branched, and contracts involuntarily and rhythmically to pump blood. The source describes a single nucleus.','Smooth muscle occurs in walls of hollow organs and vessels. Its spindle-shaped cells have a central nucleus, lack striations and are involuntary.'],'Skeletal: voluntary body movement; cardiac: involuntary heart pumping; smooth: involuntary action in hollow organs and vessels.'),
solved('v','Explain the ultra-structure of human skeletal muscles.',['A skeletal muscle contains bundles of long muscle fibres. Each fibre has a sarcolemma surrounding sarcoplasm with peripheral nuclei, mitochondria, glycogen and myoglobin.','T tubules carry excitation inward, while the sarcoplasmic reticulum stores and releases calcium.','The fibre contains myofibrils composed of repeating sarcomeres between successive Z lines.','Thin actin filaments and thick myosin filaments form the banding pattern; thin filaments also contain troponin and tropomyosin.','The A band corresponds to the thick-filament length, the I band contains thin filaments without thick overlap, and the H zone is the central thick-only region. The M line lies centrally.'],'Skeletal muscle is organized as muscle → bundles → fibres → myofibrils → sarcomeres → thick and thin filaments.'),
solved('vi','Explain the sliding filament theory of contraction using appropriately labeled diagrams of a relaxed and a contracted sarcomere.',['Calcium exposes binding sites through the thin-filament regulatory proteins.','Myosin cross-bridges bind actin and pull thin filaments toward the centre. ATP supports repeated attachment, movement and detachment.','The Z lines move closer together; the sarcomere, I bands and H zone shorten. The A band and individual filament lengths remain essentially unchanged.','When excitation stops and calcium returns to the sarcoplasmic reticulum, active cross-bridge cycling stops and the fibre can relax.'],'Contraction results from greater overlap of actin and myosin, not shortening of the filaments themselves.'),
solved('vii','Describe the structure of a sarcomere and indicate the relationship of the sarcomere to the myofilament',['A sarcomere extends from one Z line to the next and is a repeating contractile unit in a myofibril.','Thin actin filaments attach to the Z lines and extend toward the centre; thick myosin filaments occupy the central region.','Their arrangement creates I bands, an A band, an H zone and an M line.','Myofilaments are the protein filaments within each sarcomere; many sarcomeres arranged end to end form a myofibril.'],'A sarcomere is a structural unit built from overlapping thick and thin myofilaments, bounded by two Z lines.')]

def sarcomere_diagram():
 svg='<svg viewBox="0 0 600 300" role="img" aria-label="Relaxed and contracted sarcomeres: Z lines approach while actin and myosin retain their lengths" style="width:100%;max-width:720px;background:#fff;color:#142838" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><style>text{font:14px sans-serif;fill:#142838}</style>'
 for y,left,right,label in [(55,70,530,'Relaxed'),(180,120,480,'Contracted')]:
  svg+=f'<text x="20" y="{y-25}">{label}</text><path d="M{left} {y-10}v65 M{right} {y-10}v65" stroke="#182e43" stroke-width="4"/>'
  for yy in [y,y+30]:svg+=f'<path d="M{left} {yy}h170 M{right} {yy}h-170" stroke="#168eab" stroke-width="5"/>'
  svg+=f'<path d="M200 {y+15}h200" stroke="#b83c60" stroke-width="10"/><text x="{left-8}" y="{y+76}">Z</text><text x="{right-8}" y="{y+76}">Z</text><text x="283" y="{y+45}">M line</text>'
 svg+='<text x="35" y="290">Blue: thin actin</text><text x="220" y="290">Red: thick myosin</text><text x="430" y="290">Z–Z = sarcomere</text></svg>'
 return '<figure>'+svg+'<figcaption>SpaceBook schematic: overlap increases during contraction; filament lengths stay constant.</figcaption></figure>'
LONG[5]['answer']+=sarcomere_diagram()
LONG[5]['answer']+='<p>Mechanism reference: <a href="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/10-3-muscle-fiber-contraction-and-relaxation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenStax — muscle contraction and relaxation</a>.</p>'
ACTIVITIES=[]
def activity(title,answer,page=82,source=None):
 desc='<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> '+escape(answer)+'</p>'
 if source:desc+='<p>Source: <a href="'+source[1]+'" target="_blank" rel="noopener">'+escape(source[0])+'</a>.</p>'
 ACTIVITIES.append(dict(title=title,desc=desc,sourceReferences=[dict(printedPage=page)],solutionOrigin='SpaceBook learning answer'))
activity('Identify the bones of the pelvic girdle, pectoral girdle, arms and leg by using the model of human skeleton.','On the model, identify the paired hip bones (ilium, ischium and pubis), scapulae and clavicles, humerus, radius, ulna, femur, tibia and fibula. Then locate wrist, palm and finger bones and ankle, foot and toe bones. Use the model’s labels to distinguish the girdles from the free limbs.')
activity('Compare the structure of skeleton, smooth and cardiac muscles with the help of prepared slides.','Record the actual slides: skeletal muscle has long parallel striated fibres and multiple peripheral nuclei; cardiac muscle has branching striated cells and intercalated discs; smooth muscle has spindle-shaped cells, a central nucleus and no striations. Do not invent features that are not visible at the available magnification.')
activity('Draw a diagram of sarcomere and label its parts.','Draw two Z lines, thin actin filaments attached to each, and central thick myosin filaments. Label the sarcomere, A band, I bands, H zone, M line and the overlap region. The relaxed/contracted schematic in long answer vi provides a starting layout.')
activity('Justify how the main functions of the skeleton are to act as a system of rods and levers, which are moved by the muscles.','Bones act as rigid levers, joints provide pivot points and contracting muscles apply pulling forces through their attachments. The resulting lever movement changes the position of body parts. The skeleton also supports and protects the body.')
activity('Justify why do the muscles pull but do not push.','Muscle contraction develops tension that pulls on attachments. Active shortening does not push those attachments apart. Opposing muscles or external forces produce the reverse movement.')
activity('Relate the bipedal posture of man with his skeleton and musculature.','The vertebral column and pelvis support an upright trunk, the lower limbs bear weight, and the feet provide a base of support. Coordinated trunk, hip and leg muscles maintain balance and produce walking movements.',83)
activity('Name the techniques for joint transplantation.','The intended term is joint replacement or arthroplasty: partial replacement replaces part of a damaged joint, while total replacement replaces the relevant joint surfaces with artificial components. These are prosthetic replacements, rather than routine transplantation of an entire donor joint.',83,('NIAMS — osteoarthritis surgery','https://www.niams.nih.gov/health-topics/osteoarthritis/diagnosis-treatment-and-steps-to-take'))
activity('Justify the use of calcium in teenage and twenties can be a preventive action against osteoporosis.','Adequate calcium and vitamin D support development of peak bone mass during growth and early adulthood. Building stronger bones provides a better reserve against later bone loss. Nutrition is one factor alongside physical activity and other influences; the question does not imply that everyone needs supplements.',83,('NIAMS — osteoporosis and bone health','https://www.niams.nih.gov/health-topics/osteoporosis/diagnosis-treatment-and-steps-to-take'))
activity('Reason out the rigor mortis.','After death, ATP production stops. Without fresh ATP, myosin cannot detach normally from actin, so cross-bridges remain attached and the muscles become stiff.',83,('OpenStax — muscle contraction and relaxation','https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/10-3-muscle-fiber-contraction-and-relaxation'))
activity('Relate improper posture to bone/joint problems.','Posture changes how body weight and muscle forces are distributed. Sustained awkward positioning can increase strain on particular muscles, ligaments and joints. Balanced alignment and changing position help distribute loads; posture alone does not explain every skeletal disorder.',83)
